1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:01,567
Hi everyone,

2
00:00:01,587 --> 00:00:07,492
and welcome back to Sewing Machine Design and Manufacturing at WenQuest.

3
00:00:07,892 --> 00:00:14,152
This is Lecture 25, making housings and cams.

4
00:00:14,552 --> 00:00:19,413
The housing is the largest part, carrying every shaft bore;

5
00:00:19,433 --> 00:00:22,309
the cam is the most shape-critical,

6
00:00:22,329 --> 00:00:28,267
where a few microns of error become a large acceleration error at speed.

7
00:00:28,287 --> 00:00:34,142
Today we see how these parts are made accurate, how they stay accurate,

8
00:00:34,162 --> 00:00:39,520
and why a cam that passes inspection can still be noisy at speed.

9
00:00:41,040 --> 00:00:44,155
Here's a shop-floor problem.

10
00:00:44,555 --> 00:00:50,290
A batch of chainstitch looper cams has been compensation-ground,

11
00:00:50,310 --> 00:00:56,855
and the lift error is about 5 microns peak to valley, within the drawing.

12
00:00:57,255 --> 00:01:05,914
Stroke 6 millimetres, a 150 degree rise, modified trapezoid,

13
00:01:05,934 --> 00:01:08,952
top speed 6000 r/min.

14
00:01:09,352 --> 00:01:14,309
Yet once fitted, the machines are clearly noisier at speed,

15
00:01:14,329 --> 00:01:18,612
and the looper-carrier ball joints soon wear slack.

16
00:01:19,012 --> 00:01:24,863
Harmonic analysis finds an order-24 component of about 2 microns:

17
00:01:24,883 --> 00:01:27,392
chatter left by the grinder.

18
00:01:27,792 --> 00:01:30,689
The plant's rule: at top speed,

19
00:01:30,709 --> 00:01:37,369
the acceleration error must stay within 10 percent of the nominal peak.

20
00:01:37,769 --> 00:01:41,587
How fast can this cam run?

21
00:01:41,987 --> 00:01:44,887
Would another round of compensation fix it?

22
00:01:45,287 --> 00:01:46,427
We'll answer at the end.

23
00:01:47,947 --> 00:01:51,487
Housings: material, casting and ageing.

24
00:01:53,007 --> 00:01:56,796
Housings are complex, with many cavities,

25
00:01:56,816 --> 00:02:00,605
so the blanks are nearly always castings.

26
00:02:01,005 --> 00:02:07,172
Industrial lockstitch housings have traditionally been sand-cast grey iron:

27
00:02:07,192 --> 00:02:11,380
flake graphite gives it high damping, a dull sound,

28
00:02:11,400 --> 00:02:14,514
and it expands about as much as steel.

29
00:02:14,914 --> 00:02:21,754
Die-cast aluminium is light and near net shape, but the dies are expensive,

30
00:02:21,774 --> 00:02:27,150
so it suits domestic machines made by the hundred thousand.

31
00:02:27,550 --> 00:02:30,965
Aluminium expands twice as much as steel.

32
00:02:31,365 --> 00:02:39,421
Warm the housing by 30 kelvin and a 20 millimetre bore grows 13.8 microns

33
00:02:39,441 --> 00:02:43,736
while the steel bushing grows only 6.9:

34
00:02:43,756 --> 00:02:47,940
the interference drops by 6.9 microns.

35
00:02:48,340 --> 00:02:53,035
So bushing fits in aluminium housings must be checked hot.

36
00:02:54,555 --> 00:02:57,609
We can estimate the size of it.

37
00:02:58,009 --> 00:03:06,330
Treat the arm as a box cantilever from the pillar: 250 millimetres long,

38
00:03:06,350 --> 00:03:10,847
a 60 by 70 section, 7 millimetre walls.

39
00:03:11,247 --> 00:03:17,527
Suppose the front wall's 2 millimetre skin carries an unbalanced residual

40
00:03:17,547 --> 00:03:20,979
stress sigma that the back wall doesn't.

41
00:03:21,379 --> 00:03:25,649
Its resultant times the lever arm is a moment;

42
00:03:25,669 --> 00:03:28,820
the moment over EI is a curvature;

43
00:03:28,840 --> 00:03:36,562
curvature times L squared over two is how far the needle-bar bore at the tip moves.

44
00:03:36,962 --> 00:03:42,752
Release all of 40 megapascals and that's about 52 microns.

45
00:03:43,152 --> 00:03:51,192
At room temperature, assume half is released with a three-month time constant:

46
00:03:51,212 --> 00:03:54,292
about 26 microns after a year.

47
00:03:54,692 --> 00:03:58,955
Aged down to 10 megapascals, only about 6.

48
00:03:59,355 --> 00:04:03,815
The hook gap allows just plus or minus 30.

49
00:04:05,335 --> 00:04:10,035
Bores: datums, one set-up and dimension chains.

50
00:04:11,555 --> 00:04:14,855
A housing's bores lie on several faces.

51
00:04:15,255 --> 00:04:22,612
Re-clamp for every face and each set-up adds a few to a few tens of microns

52
00:04:22,632 --> 00:04:24,382
of location error.

53
00:04:24,782 --> 00:04:31,881
A horizontal machining centre indexes its table about the B axis: clamp once,

54
00:04:31,901 --> 00:04:38,075
turn to 0, 90, 180 and 270 degrees, and machine four faces in turn,

55
00:04:38,095 --> 00:04:43,530
so the bore pattern depends only on the machine's accuracy.

56
00:04:43,930 --> 00:04:50,946
Bores on one axis are best line-bored with one long bar in a single pass,

57
00:04:50,966 --> 00:04:54,030
coaxial to about 3 to 5 microns.

58
00:04:54,430 --> 00:05:00,715
If the bar can't reach, you have to turn round, which adds two errors:

59
00:05:00,735 --> 00:05:04,138
twice the centring error of the table,

60
00:05:04,158 --> 00:05:09,452
plus the indexing error times the distance from the centre.

61
00:05:09,852 --> 00:05:14,352
In our example that's about 14 microns.

62
00:05:14,752 --> 00:05:19,277
So the designer makes coaxial bores step down from one end,

63
00:05:19,297 --> 00:05:23,052
or keeps them equal, so one bar can pass through.

64
00:05:24,572 --> 00:05:27,912
Here's a datum mismatch.

65
00:05:28,312 --> 00:05:35,032
The drawing gives 32 plus or minus 0.03 millimetres from the hook-shaft bore

66
00:05:35,052 --> 00:05:37,427
axis to the top of the bed,

67
00:05:37,447 --> 00:05:43,901
because the hook height and the needle plate are measured from that face.

68
00:05:44,301 --> 00:05:49,651
But the housing is machined from face A underneath:

69
00:05:49,671 --> 00:05:56,601
milling the bed top gives A1, boring the hook-shaft bore gives A2.

70
00:05:57,001 --> 00:06:04,333
H0 isn't machined directly; it's A1 minus A2, the closing link.

71
00:06:04,733 --> 00:06:10,783
With A1 at 115 plus or minus 0.02, A2 is nominally 83,

72
00:06:10,803 --> 00:06:18,201
and the worst-case method leaves it only plus or minus 10 microns;

73
00:06:18,221 --> 00:06:23,484
the statistical method allows plus or minus 22.

74
00:06:23,884 --> 00:06:28,859
Give the boring the full 0.03 without converting,

75
00:06:28,879 --> 00:06:32,224
and only about 98.7 percent pass.

76
00:06:32,624 --> 00:06:36,646
Better still, make the datums coincide:

77
00:06:36,666 --> 00:06:44,626
locate or gauge the boring from the bed top, and all 0.03 goes to the boring.

78
00:06:46,146 --> 00:06:49,213
Cams: motion laws and how they're made.

79
00:06:50,733 --> 00:06:57,815
A cam turns the steady rotation of the shaft into a reciprocating motion that

80
00:06:57,835 --> 00:06:59,752
follows a chosen law:

81
00:06:59,772 --> 00:07:03,902
take-up, feed-lift eccentrics, looper drives,

82
00:07:03,922 --> 00:07:07,223
zigzag and pattern cams all use one.

83
00:07:07,623 --> 00:07:12,855
If the stroke h is completed over a shaft angle beta,

84
00:07:12,875 --> 00:07:19,495
the peak acceleration is C times h omega squared over beta squared,

85
00:07:19,515 --> 00:07:24,153
and C is about 4.89 for the modified trapezoid.

86
00:07:24,553 --> 00:07:29,854
Our looper cam has a 20 millimetre base radius,

87
00:07:29,874 --> 00:07:37,891
a 6 millimetre roller and a 6 millimetre stroke over a 150 degree rise.

88
00:07:38,291 --> 00:07:48,990
At 6000 r/min the nominal peak is about 1690 metres per second squared,

89
00:07:49,010 --> 00:07:53,671
so the 10 percent limit is 169.

90
00:07:54,071 --> 00:08:00,529
On the right is the cam from the virtual lab, with the actual profile in red,

91
00:08:00,549 --> 00:08:07,091
error magnified: the standard case, then with order-24 chatter added, in turn.

92
00:08:08,611 --> 00:08:12,336
How much distortion can grinding take out?

93
00:08:12,736 --> 00:08:15,436
The idea here is error replication.

94
00:08:15,836 --> 00:08:22,963
Where there's more stock the cutting force is higher and the wheel and work

95
00:08:22,983 --> 00:08:24,202
deflect more;

96
00:08:24,222 --> 00:08:27,728
where there's less they deflect less.

97
00:08:28,128 --> 00:08:33,028
So each operation's error is copied, scaled down, into the next.

98
00:08:33,428 --> 00:08:37,537
The replication factor epsilon is below one,

99
00:08:37,557 --> 00:08:44,202
and every extra pass shrinks the shape error to epsilon times the last.

100
00:08:44,602 --> 00:08:47,844
Take 0.25: after carburising,

101
00:08:47,864 --> 00:08:56,054
a second order error of 20 microns becomes 5 after one finishing pass and

102
00:08:56,074 --> 00:08:57,742
1.25 after two.

103
00:08:58,142 --> 00:09:03,452
More passes, more accuracy, but more time and more wheel dressing:

104
00:09:03,472 --> 00:09:06,522
a trade-off between cost and accuracy.

105
00:09:08,042 --> 00:09:11,552
A CNC cam grinder moves two axes together:

106
00:09:11,572 --> 00:09:17,183
the C axis turns the cam and the X axis moves the wheel in and out,

107
00:09:17,203 --> 00:09:24,075
so the wheel centre traces the actual profile offset outwards by the wheel radius.

108
00:09:24,475 --> 00:09:29,589
Every dressing makes the wheel a little smaller.

109
00:09:29,989 --> 00:09:34,076
The control computes the path from the stored radius,

110
00:09:34,096 --> 00:09:38,649
so after dressing you must measure the wheel and update it.

111
00:09:39,049 --> 00:09:42,744
If it's really d smaller and nobody updates it,

112
00:09:42,764 --> 00:09:48,989
the wheel stays d away everywhere, and the lift is too big by d over cos alpha.

113
00:09:49,389 --> 00:09:52,778
With d at 0.2 millimetres,

114
00:09:52,798 --> 00:10:02,089
the size error is about 201 microns but the shape error only about 2.5.

115
00:10:02,489 --> 00:10:05,908
It's mainly a size problem, invisible in the shape;

116
00:10:05,928 --> 00:10:10,764
only measuring after every dressing, and checking the first part's size,

117
00:10:10,784 --> 00:10:11,709
will catch it.

118
00:10:13,229 --> 00:10:16,209
Error orders and compensation grinding.

119
00:10:17,729 --> 00:10:20,768
Why do a few microns matter so much?

120
00:10:21,168 --> 00:10:24,148
Break the lift error into harmonics.

121
00:10:24,548 --> 00:10:26,204
The mean is the size error.

122
00:10:26,604 --> 00:10:32,625
Orders one to three come from eccentricity, heat treatment and clamping,

123
00:10:32,645 --> 00:10:39,084
and they shift the lift: positions such as loop-taking timing and clearances.

124
00:10:39,484 --> 00:10:42,682
High orders come from grinder vibration,

125
00:10:42,702 --> 00:10:47,186
wheel imbalance and axis following error; they're small.

126
00:10:47,586 --> 00:10:50,222
With the cam turning steadily,

127
00:10:50,242 --> 00:10:56,510
the acceleration error is the second time derivative of the lift error,

128
00:10:56,530 --> 00:11:01,646
and order k has an amplitude of e k times k omega squared.

129
00:11:02,046 --> 00:11:12,630
At 6000 r/min, each micron gives under 0.4 at order one, about 14 at order six,

130
00:11:12,650 --> 00:11:17,462
227 at order 24 and 512 at order 36.

131
00:11:17,862 --> 00:11:27,602
Two microns at order 24 alone give 455, 27 percent of the nominal peak.

132
00:11:29,122 --> 00:11:34,694
This is the lab's curve of peak acceleration error against speed.

133
00:11:35,094 --> 00:11:43,572
The yellow dashed line is the 169 limit and the vertical line is the 6000

134
00:11:43,592 --> 00:11:45,434
r/min top speed.

135
00:11:45,834 --> 00:11:49,661
In the standard case the curve stays under the limit.

136
00:11:50,061 --> 00:11:54,105
Add the order-24 chatter and the whole curve jumps up;

137
00:11:54,125 --> 00:11:58,321
where it crosses the limit is the highest allowed speed.

138
00:11:58,721 --> 00:12:02,498
Because the error grows with the square of speed,

139
00:12:02,518 --> 00:12:08,233
the allowed speed is the original speed times the square root of the limit

140
00:12:08,253 --> 00:12:09,938
over the actual error.

141
00:12:11,458 --> 00:12:16,723
Once you've measured the lift error, you can add it, reversed,

142
00:12:16,743 --> 00:12:21,838
to the grinding path and grind again: compensation grinding.

143
00:12:22,238 --> 00:12:24,595
It never cleans up completely.

144
00:12:24,995 --> 00:12:28,483
The gauge has error, the machine has error,

145
00:12:28,503 --> 00:12:33,541
and the wheel touches the profile over an arc with some width,

146
00:12:33,561 --> 00:12:38,109
which acts like a low-pass filter: the higher the order,

147
00:12:38,129 --> 00:12:40,394
the harder it is to correct.

148
00:12:40,794 --> 00:12:46,156
In our model one round removes 84 percent of order two,

149
00:12:46,176 --> 00:12:51,734
78 percent of order six, and only 20 percent of order 24.

150
00:12:52,134 --> 00:12:59,258
On the right, dashed is as ground and solid is after one round:

151
00:12:59,278 --> 00:13:06,969
the default looper cam goes from 16.6 to 3.0 microns peak to valley.

152
00:13:08,489 --> 00:13:16,295
On a CNC cam grinder the C axis turns and the X axis follows:

153
00:13:16,315 --> 00:13:18,989
X is a function of C.

154
00:13:19,389 --> 00:13:25,948
That's exactly a robot arm using an electronic cam to make a joint follow a

155
00:13:25,968 --> 00:13:27,088
master angle.

156
00:13:27,488 --> 00:13:30,638
In a robot's path, interpolation ripple,

157
00:13:30,658 --> 00:13:35,869
encoder sub-division error and the twice-per-revolution error of a

158
00:13:35,889 --> 00:13:39,832
strain-wave reducer are all high-order components,

159
00:13:39,852 --> 00:13:46,172
and the second derivative magnifies them into ripple in acceleration and torque,

160
00:13:46,192 --> 00:13:48,788
seen as tool-tip shake and noise.

161
00:13:49,188 --> 00:13:55,945
That's why robot paths use modified-trapezoid or S-curve profiles,

162
00:13:55,965 --> 00:14:02,723
and why servo tuning must look specifically for high-order ripple.

163
00:14:03,123 --> 00:14:09,046
And a robot joint housing's bearing and reducer bores must be bored in one set-up,

164
00:14:09,066 --> 00:14:11,583
just like a sewing machine housing.

165
00:14:13,103 --> 00:14:15,215
You've felt this on the road.

166
00:14:15,615 --> 00:14:21,810
A pothole a few centimetres deep is barely noticed at low speed but jolts

167
00:14:21,830 --> 00:14:23,428
hard at high speed:

168
00:14:23,448 --> 00:14:30,155
the same amplitude, but the body's acceleration grows with the square of speed.

169
00:14:30,555 --> 00:14:35,591
A washboard road shakes you more than one long gentle slope,

170
00:14:35,611 --> 00:14:38,793
because its ripples are of high order,

171
00:14:38,813 --> 00:14:43,175
and acceleration grows with the square of the order.

172
00:14:43,575 --> 00:14:47,875
Chatter on a cam profile is a tiny washboard road.

173
00:14:49,395 --> 00:14:51,295
Back to the opening problem.

174
00:14:51,695 --> 00:15:03,691
Omega is 628, so the nominal peak is about 1690 and the limit 169.

175
00:15:04,091 --> 00:15:12,888
Two microns at order 24 alone give 455, 2.7 times the limit.

176
00:15:13,288 --> 00:15:19,361
With the other errors included it's about 469 as ground;

177
00:15:19,381 --> 00:15:26,869
on a 60 gram looper carrier that's an extra 28 newtons at 2400 hertz,

178
00:15:26,889 --> 00:15:32,091
the source of the noise and the ball-joint wear,

179
00:15:32,111 --> 00:15:37,205
and the criterion allows only about 3600 r/min.

180
00:15:37,605 --> 00:15:47,545
One round of compensation brings it to 366, still only about 4080 r/min.

181
00:15:47,945 --> 00:15:50,584
More compensation won't do,

182
00:15:50,604 --> 00:15:55,804
since each round removes only 20 percent of order 24.

183
00:15:56,204 --> 00:16:00,537
Remove it in the process: balance and dress the wheel,

184
00:16:00,557 --> 00:16:06,744
make the grinder stiffer and better damped, and finish with spark-out passes.

185
00:16:08,264 --> 00:16:13,393
Now Virtual lab 25-1, cam profile error and compensation grinding,

186
00:16:13,413 --> 00:16:19,167
which you can do yourself on WenQuest. The criteria and tasks are blurred.

187
00:16:19,187 --> 00:16:25,253
On the left: the magnified profile error, the lift-error curve, the harmonics,

188
00:16:25,273 --> 00:16:31,339
and acceleration error against speed. On the right you set the heat treatment,

189
00:16:31,359 --> 00:16:36,644
the finishing passes, a machine-error harmonic and the wheel radius.

190
00:16:37,044 --> 00:16:42,849
Task one: in the standard case, click 'Compensation grind one round'.

191
00:16:42,869 --> 00:16:49,264
Use the harmonic chart to see which orders drop a lot and which barely move.

192
00:16:49,664 --> 00:16:56,318
Task two: drag the dressed wheel radius to 199.80 and untick the radius update,

193
00:16:56,338 --> 00:17:02,569
then compensate once and see whether the size error can pass. If it can't,

194
00:17:02,589 --> 00:17:07,469
why not, and what should come first, is for you to answer.

195
00:17:07,869 --> 00:17:12,310
Task three: reset and press Chatter, order 24, 2 microns,

196
00:17:12,330 --> 00:17:17,868
and watch the acceleration curve rise. After one round of compensation,

197
00:17:17,888 --> 00:17:21,077
read the acceleration error at top speed,

198
00:17:21,097 --> 00:17:26,009
work out the allowed top speed yourself, and enter it to check.

199
00:17:26,409 --> 00:17:30,593
Finally switch to the take-up cam with the same chatter,

200
00:17:30,613 --> 00:17:35,022
and work out why its acceleration error is so much smaller.

201
00:17:35,042 --> 00:17:38,776
The guide and report template are on the platform.

202
00:17:40,296 --> 00:17:45,999
This is the WenQuest digital factory. The assembly line measures every machine,

203
00:17:46,019 --> 00:17:50,997
and each reading travels over the bus with its serial number into the

204
00:17:51,017 --> 00:17:53,171
historian and a control chart,

205
00:17:53,191 --> 00:17:56,358
which alarms as soon as the process changes.

206
00:17:56,378 --> 00:18:01,791
The CMM and the cam gauge from today's lecture send one measurement message

207
00:18:01,811 --> 00:18:05,196
per characteristic along exactly the same path.

208
00:18:05,596 --> 00:18:09,104
Switch to the trace page. Besides the hook batch,

209
00:18:09,124 --> 00:18:13,569
each machine's record holds the housing's casting heat number:

210
00:18:13,589 --> 00:18:16,377
which melt its housing was poured from.

211
00:18:16,397 --> 00:18:19,617
If a heat of housings ever missed its ageing,

212
00:18:19,637 --> 00:18:23,865
that number would lead you to every machine they went into.

213
00:18:24,265 --> 00:18:29,993
Drag the serial number and look through the machine records one by one.

214
00:18:30,013 --> 00:18:36,226
The next two lectures walk through connected machines and the digital factory

215
00:18:36,246 --> 00:18:36,873
in full.

216
00:18:38,393 --> 00:18:41,508
Your assignment has four questions. One:

217
00:18:41,528 --> 00:18:46,994
a steel bushing in a 30 millimetre bore of an aluminium housing has 10

218
00:18:47,014 --> 00:18:49,267
microns of cold interference.

219
00:18:49,287 --> 00:18:53,734
After a 35 kelvin rise, how much is left, will it loosen,

220
00:18:53,754 --> 00:18:55,693
and how would you fix it?

221
00:18:56,093 --> 00:19:02,241
Two: a design dimension of 28 plus or minus 0.03 is held by two operations.

222
00:19:02,261 --> 00:19:08,490
Convert the operational tolerance by the worst-case and statistical methods,

223
00:19:08,510 --> 00:19:12,273
and say what changes once the datums coincide.

224
00:19:12,673 --> 00:19:17,863
Three: a looper cam has harmonics at orders 2, 3, 8 and 24.

225
00:19:17,883 --> 00:19:24,839
Work out each acceleration error, judge whether it passes, how fast it may run,

226
00:19:24,859 --> 00:19:28,195
and which order to tackle first. Four:

227
00:19:28,215 --> 00:19:31,816
the shop wants to drop the second ageing;

228
00:19:31,836 --> 00:19:35,613
write an opinion of no more than 300 words.

229
00:19:36,013 --> 00:19:40,833
Submit the assignment and the lab report on the WenQuest platform.

230
00:19:42,353 --> 00:19:46,013
There's also a ten-question quiz. For example:

231
00:19:46,033 --> 00:19:51,853
a steel bushing sits in a 25 millimetre bore of an aluminium housing that

232
00:19:51,873 --> 00:19:53,373
warms by 40 kelvin.

233
00:19:53,393 --> 00:19:56,732
How many microns of interference are lost?

234
00:19:57,132 --> 00:20:04,625
Or true or false: if every housing passes on the CMM after machining,

235
00:20:04,645 --> 00:20:09,415
that proves skipping ageing carries no risk.

236
00:20:09,815 --> 00:20:15,160
You get three attempts, and the explanations appear as soon as you submit.

237
00:20:16,680 --> 00:20:18,300
Three takeaways.

238
00:20:18,700 --> 00:20:22,077
One: the housing fixes positions.

239
00:20:22,477 --> 00:20:28,435
Grey iron is well damped and expands like steel; aluminium is light,

240
00:20:28,455 --> 00:20:32,039
but its bushing fits must be checked hot.

241
00:20:32,439 --> 00:20:37,829
Residual stress can move the needle-bar bore by tens of microns,

242
00:20:37,849 --> 00:20:41,379
and ageing decides whether accuracy lasts.

243
00:20:41,779 --> 00:20:47,027
Two: bore accuracy comes from common datums, one face and two pins,

244
00:20:47,047 --> 00:20:51,587
and boring in one set-up on a horizontal machining centre;

245
00:20:51,607 --> 00:20:57,799
when datums don't coincide, convert the tolerances through the dimension chain.

246
00:20:58,199 --> 00:21:05,706
Three: cams are shaped before hardening and made accurate by grinding after,

247
00:21:05,726 --> 00:21:12,539
and order k gives an acceleration error of e k times k omega squared.

248
00:21:12,939 --> 00:21:19,279
Low orders are easy to fix; high orders must be removed in the process.

249
00:21:19,679 --> 00:21:22,619
The slides, the virtual lab,

250
00:21:22,639 --> 00:21:30,017
the assignment and the quiz are all on the WenQuest learning platform,

251
00:21:30,037 --> 00:21:37,099
at learn.wenquestrobotics.com. Sign up for free and start learning.

252
00:21:37,499 --> 00:21:38,559
See you next time.
